Folio (test build 0.0.1-test71)
===============================

A reader for PDF and ebook files (EPUB, FB2, MOBI, CBZ, XPS), built on
MuPDF. This is an early test: it opens and shows documents, selects and
copies text, and looks words up. Markup, notebooks and the rest come later.

The bookshelf
  Click Select on Folio's icon bar icon (or choose Bookshelf... from its
  menu) to see your bookshelf: the covers of your books, with how far
  you've read each. Double-click a cover to open it.
  To add books, drag them to the bookshelf, or choose On bookshelf from a
  document's menu. Drag a directory to the bookshelf to have Folio watch
  it: Folio looks in it, and the directories inside, for new documents
  each time it starts and when you choose Refresh from the bookshelf's
  menu. Watched folders... on that menu lists them (click Stop watching to
  drop one).
  Remove from bookshelf (or Delete) only takes a book off the shelf; the
  file isn't touched. Books whose files can't be found are shown grey;
  Remove missing books takes them all off. Sort by recent, title or
  author from the menu. The shelf is kept in <Choices$Write>.Folio.Shelf
  and the covers in <Choices$Write>.Folio.Covers.

Opening a document
  Drag a file to Folio's icon on the icon bar, or double-click a PDF while
  Folio is running. A document that is already open comes to the front.
  If a PDF needs a password, Folio asks for it. Dragging several files at
  once opens each in its own window; to join them into one PDF, use
  Create PDF (below). Drag a directory of pictures there instead to make
  a PDF of them (see "Pictures to a PDF").

In a document window
  Scroll as usual. Page Down / Page Up    next / previous page
  Home / End     first / last page        Up / Down   scroll a little
  Ctrl-N         dark page on and off     Ctrl-F2     close the window
  F4             Find                     Ctrl-Left   back from a link
  Print          Print...                 F5          Go to page
  Menu           View: Dark page, Sepia page, Zoom in, Zoom out,
                 Actual size, Fit width, Two pages. Navigate: the page
                 moves above, Go to page, Contents and Back. Find... and
                 Define "word".
  Close with Adjust also opens the document's directory.
  F11 (or View > Full screen) fills the screen with the document, with no
  title bar, scroll bars or toolbar; F11 or Escape returns to the desktop.
  Pages of different sizes are each shown at their own size (a book
  whose cover is smaller than its pages, a landscape page among upright
  ones). Folio measures the pages in the background after opening.
  Default scale: zoom a window to the scale you like, then choose View >
  Use this scale for new documents. Every document opens at that scale
  from then on (also in the Choices window).

The toolbar and thumbnails
  The toolbar along the top of each document window has, from the left:
  thumbnails on/off, Contents, previous page, the page number (type a
  number and press Return to go there), next page, zoom out, the scale,
  zoom in, fit width, two pages, dark page, Find, Define, Highlight, Underline,
  Strike out and Note (and Read aloud, with the Speech plugin). Rest the
  pointer on a button for a moment to see its name. View > Toolbar hides
  or shows it.
  Windows open wide enough for every button. If you make one narrower,
  arrows appear at the toolbar's right end: click them (Adjust goes the
  other way), or use the mouse wheel over the toolbar, to scroll it.
  The thumbnails drawer (View > Thumbnails, or the first toolbar button)
  shows small pages down the left of the window, which widens to make room
  for it (and narrows again when it closes); the page you're reading
  is framed in blue, and a click on a thumbnail goes to that page.

Two pages side by side
  View > Two pages (or the two-pages toolbar button) shows the pages in
  pairs; the window widens to make room, and narrows again when you go
  back to one. Page Down and Page Up then move a pair at a time. With
  First page alone ticked (as it is to start with), the first page sits
  on its own on the right and the rest pair up as in a printed book; untick
  it for pages 1 and 2 side by side.

Notes and markings (PDFs)
  Select some text, then click Highlight, Underline or Strike out on the
  toolbar (or use the Edit menu). A selection over several pages is marked
  on each of them (Ctrl-Z then undoes one page at a time). Note adds a
  note at the selection, or at the top of the page; click on a note to
  read, change or delete it. Click on a highlight to select it; Delete
  removes it.
  Edit > Colour chooses the colour of new markings: yellow, green, blue,
  pink, red, orange, purple or grey, or the usual colours (yellow
  highlights and notes, blue underlines, red strike-outs). Folio remembers
  it. If you've clicked on an annotation first, it changes to that colour
  too.
  Navigate > Annotations... lists every annotation in the document,
  including those made in other programs: its page, its colour, what kind
  it is, the note's text or the text it marks, and who made it. Click on
  one to go to it and select it. The list keeps up as you make changes.
  Ctrl-Z undoes the last change and Ctrl-Y redoes it. F3 (or Save... on
  the menu) saves the PDF with its notes and markings; the window's title
  shows * while there are changes to save, and closing the window or
  quitting asks first. Ebooks can't be marked yet.

Reading aloud (with the Speech plugin)
  Drag the !Folio in FolioSpeech-<version>.zip over this !Folio (the Filer
  adds Plugins.Speech to it), then run Folio again. A speaker button
  then appears at the right of the toolbar, and Read aloud, Speak selection
  and Reading speed on the document's menu. Without the plugin there's no
  speech, and nothing for it is loaded. Speech.ReadMe says what it needs
  (SharedSoundBuffer and StreamManager among them).
  Read aloud starts at the top of what you can see of the page, or at the
  sentence the selection is in, and goes on through the document; the
  sentence being read is shown with a tinted band, and the pages turn as it
  goes. Click the button (or Read aloud) again to stop. Speak selection
  reads just the selected text.

Scanned pages (with the OCR plugin)
  A scanned PDF is pictures of pages, with no text to find or select.
  Drag the !Folio in FolioOCR-<version>.zip over this !Folio (the Filer
  adds Plugins.OCR to it), then run Folio again: the document's menu gets
  Text recognition. Folio then reads the text in each scanned page as you
  come to it (the window's title says when), and Find, selecting, Copy,
  Define and Read aloud work on it. Recognise all pages does the whole
  document in the background, so Find can search all of it; Recognise this
  page does just the page you're on. Accurate makes fewer mistakes; Fast
  takes about half as long. What's recognised is kept, so it isn't done
  again next time. OCR.ReadMe has more.
  Add recognised text to the PDF (on the Text recognition menu) makes a
  searchable copy: the recognised words go into the PDF itself as
  invisible text over their pictures, the way scanning programs do it, so
  other programs (and other readers) can find, select and copy them too.
  The pages look just the same. Save (F3) to keep it; Ctrl-Z takes it out
  again. Do it once every page has been recognised.

Filling in forms (PDFs)
  Click on a field to fill it in. A text field opens for typing over the
  page: Return or Tab keeps what you type (Tab then goes to the next
  field, Shift-Tab back), Escape leaves it as it was. In a field of several
  lines, Return and the arrow keys move between them. A click on a check
  box or radio button ticks it; a list or drop-down field shows its
  choices as a menu. Ctrl-Z undoes each change; F3 saves the PDF with
  what you've filled in. Fields worked out by the form (totals, dates
  checked as you type) need its JavaScript, which is off unless Choices
  turn it on; Folio never sends a form anywhere.

XFA forms (made with Adobe's form designer)
  Some PDF forms are XFA forms, made with Adobe's LiveCycle Designer: the
  form's design and its values are kept inside the PDF in Adobe's own way.
  Folio can't draw an XFA design, but it can fill one in as a list. Edit >
  Form fields... lists every field with what's in it: click on one to
  type in it, choose from its choices, or tick it. If the form has
  ordinary fields on its pages too (a "static" XFA form), filling in
  either keeps the other in step. A "dynamic" XFA form's page only says
  "Please wait..." (it needs Adobe Reader to draw it); Folio opens its
  Form fields window by itself. Save (F3) keeps what you've filled in,
  and Adobe Reader shows it when the form is opened there. Folio doesn't
  run an XFA form's scripts, so fields it works out (totals) aren't
  updated until it's opened in Adobe Reader.

Drawing, stamps and pictures (PDFs)
  Edit > Draw... opens the Draw window: drag with Select on the page to
  draw, in black, blue or red, with a thin, medium or thick pen; Adjust
  (or Undo) takes the last stroke off. Keep puts the drawing on the page;
  so does closing the window, or starting to draw on another page.
  Edit > Stamp puts a stamp on the page: choose one (Approved, Draft,
  Confidential and so on), then click where it goes. Stamp > Picture...
  puts a picture there instead (a logo, or a signature written on paper
  and scanned): drag the picture file (JPEG, PNG and so on) into the box,
  then click on the page. "Make white see-through" lets the page show
  through the paper. Escape (or Adjust) gives up placing it. Each of
  these is a note on the page: Ctrl-Z undoes it, Delete removes it once
  clicked, and a signed PDF only takes them where its signature allows
  notes. A drawn or scanned signature only looks like a signature: it
  doesn't prove who signed or that the document is unchanged (see
  Signing a PDF for that).

My signature
  Edit > Stamp > Set up my signature... keeps your handwritten signature
  for putting on documents: write it on the pad with Select (Adjust takes
  the last stroke off), or drag a picture of it (written on paper and
  scanned) into the window, and choose how big it goes on the page. Save
  keeps it in Choices. Then Edit > Stamp > My signature, and a click on
  the page, puts it there. In the Sign box, "Show my handwritten
  signature" puts it beside your name in a digital signature too.

Measuring
  Measure... on the Edit menu opens the Measure window. While it's open,
  click Select on the page to add points: red lines join them, and Adjust
  takes the last one off. The window shows the last line's length, all the
  lines together, and with three points or more the area they enclose.
  Lengths are in mm, cm, inches or points, at the page's paper size; a
  plan or drawing whose PDF gives it a scale of its own (1 in = 10 ft,
  say) is measured in its own units. Add to page keeps the measurement on
  the page as an annotation, with the figures as its note. Close the
  window to stop measuring.

Printing
  Print... on the document's menu (or the Print key) opens the Print box:
  all pages or some, copies, scale, upright or sideways, and draft. It
  needs !Printers loaded with a printer chosen. Each page is made as big as
  it really is at 100% (smaller if it wouldn't fit the paper), in the
  middle of the paper; pages are always printed white, with your notes and
  markings. Printing goes on in the background while you carry on: the
  window's title shows which page it's on. The machine only pauses while
  the printer driver takes each finished page. Choose Print... again (or
  close the window) to stop it.

Document info
  Document info... on the document's menu shows the file's name, where it
  is and its size, then what the document says about itself: title,
  author, subject, keywords, the program that made it, when it was made
  and changed, its PDF version, pages and page size, its security and what
  its author allows (printing, copying, changing, notes...), whether it
  has a form, attached files or JavaScript, how many bookmarks, layers and
  pictures it has, and every font it uses: each font's name, its kind
  (Type 1, TrueType, OpenType, Type 3...) and whether it is embedded in
  the PDF, whole or as a subset of the characters used. A font that isn't
  embedded is drawn with the nearest font the computer has. On a very
  long document Folio stops looking after a few seconds and says how many
  pages it looked at.

Restrictions (PDFs)
  A PDF's author can say it mustn't be printed, copied from or filled in.
  Folio keeps to that: Print, Copy, Export page as Draw, Save pages as
  PDF, Save page pictures, Save pages as text, filling in its form and (if
  the author doesn't allow text for screen readers either) Read aloud say
  so instead, and offer to lift it with the document's owner password. To
  have Folio ignore such restrictions, untick "Keep to a PDF's limits on
  printing and copying" in the Choices window.

Signatures (PDFs)
  A signed PDF carries a digital signature: proof that it hasn't been
  changed since it was signed, and a certificate saying who signed it.
  Folio checks it. Click on a signature on the page to see who signed it
  and when, whether the document is as it was signed, and whether the
  signer's certificate comes from a body Folio trusts. Document info lists
  every signature too. If a signature has been broken (the document
  changed after it was signed), Folio says so as the PDF opens.
  "Changed since" means changes were saved after the signature, such as a
  form filled in or notes added: what was signed is still as it was.
  Folio looks at what was changed, as Acrobat does: a certified PDF's
  signature is broken by changes the certification doesn't allow, and
  any signature by a change to a field it locks. Clicking a signature
  lists what has been changed since.
  "Signer not confirmed" means the signature is good but the certificate
  doesn't come from a body Folio trusts, so who signed it can't be
  confirmed.
  Folio trusts Adobe's root, and the certificates of CaCertificates if
  it's installed (from PackMan; the same ones web browsers trust). To
  trust others, such as your company's, put them (PEM, one after another)
  in a file called Trusted in <Choices$Write>.Folio, and run Folio again.

Signing a PDF
  To sign, you need a certificate with its private key, in a .p12 or .pfx
  file (the kind a certificate authority issues, or that other programs
  export), and its password. Choose Sign... from the document menu, or
  click an empty signature field. Drag the certificate file into the Sign
  box, type its password, and, if you like, a reason (such as "I approve
  this document") and where you are. Choose the kind: Ordinary (changes
  can still be saved after it, and are shown when it's checked), Lock the
  PDF (no changes after it; its fields can't be filled in), or Certify
  (the first signature only), allowing no changes, filling in and
  signing, or those and notes and markings. Then click Sign. A new signature goes
  at the foot of the page, on the right; an empty field is signed where it
  is. The Save box then opens: the signature is made as the PDF is saved,
  and it can't be undone before then (close the document without saving
  instead). Other signatures in the PDF stay good: changes are added to
  the end of the file. Folio keeps the certificate file's name until it
  quits, but never the password.
  Once a PDF is signed, Folio keeps the signature as it was signed: a
  signature can't be changed, cleared or signed again; the pages can't be
  turned, deleted, moved or added to; recognised text isn't written into
  them; the password can't be changed; fields a signature locks can't be
  filled in; and the PDF is only ever saved by adding to the end of it.
  A certified PDF's own rules are obeyed too (no changes at all, or only
  filling in and signing, or those and notes). Notes, markings and filling
  in other fields are still allowed where the PDF allows them, and the
  signature then says that changes were saved after it.

Security
  Folio asks before following a link out of a document (to a web page, an
  email or another file), showing where it goes; links to other programs
  are never followed. When it opens a PDF with JavaScript, attached files,
  or actions that would start a program or send the form somewhere, it
  says so; it doesn't do any of those things. A form's JavaScript runs
  only if the Choices window allows it, and can't reach your files or the
  network.

Choices
  Choices... on Folio's icon bar menu opens the Choices window: how new
  documents open (scale, page colour, two pages, the toolbar), whether
  links are underlined, the
  reading-aloud speed, text recognition (Accurate or Fast, and whether
  scanned pages are recognised as they're shown), and the security
  choices above. Save uses and keeps them (in
  <Choices$Write>.Folio.Choices); Default fills in Folio's own settings.

Fonts
  PDFs usually carry their fonts. For one that doesn't, and for scripts
  Folio has no font of its own for (Chinese, Japanese, Korean), it uses
  the computer's fonts through fontconfig: PackMan's FontConfig setup if
  it's installed, else the fonts in !Folio.Fonts (Fonts.ReadMe says more).

Passwords (PDFs)
  Password... on a PDF's menu shows whether it has a password, and puts
  one on or takes it off. Type the new password twice and click Set
  password; the Save box then opens, and the password is on the file once
  it's saved (Folio writes the whole file again, encrypted with AES-256).
  Remove password saves it without one. A PDF that anyone can open but
  that has an owner password needs that password first.
  Don't forget the password: without it, nobody can open the file.
  Setting a password needs the CryptRandom module, which Folio loads from
  !System; saving with a new password, or without one, also undoes any
  digital signatures in the file.

Export page as Draw
  Export page as Draw... on the document's menu saves the page you're
  reading as a Draw file: drag its icon to a directory, or straight into
  Draw. Lines and shapes become Draw paths; text becomes outlines (it looks
  the same without the document's fonts); each picture becomes a sprite of
  its own, at its own resolution, cut to the part that shows, so you can
  take it out and save it on its own. Gradients become sprites too.
  Draw can't clip or blend, so a few things differ from the page: see-
  through effects are lost, and highlighter marks go under the text.

Save pages as PDF
  Save pages as PDF... on the document's menu makes a new PDF of some of
  its pages. Type which: 1-10, or 1-3, 20, 45-50, or 20- for page 20 to
  the end, or all; then Save, and drag the PDF icon to a directory. A
  PDF's pages are copied just as they are (pictures, fonts, notes and
  form fields), so nothing is compressed again. Other documents' pages
  (CBZ, XPS, ebooks) are drawn into PDF pages; their JPEG pictures go in
  unchanged, so a CBZ of JPEGs becomes a PDF of the same JPEGs.

Save page pictures
  Save page pictures... saves the chosen pages as picture files in a new
  directory (drag its icon to where it should go): Page001, Page002...
  numbered as the pages are. A page that is one picture, as a scanned page
  is, gives that picture just as the PDF stores it: a JPEG comes out as
  the same JPEG, byte for byte, not drawn again (hidden recognised text on
  it doesn't matter). A page that is one picture of another kind gives
  that picture, at its own size, as a PNG. Any other page (text, or
  several pictures) is drawn at the resolution in the box, 150 dpi unless
  you change it, as a PNG. A JPEG is saved as it is stored, which is
  usually the page's way up; if the PDF turns it, the file isn't turned.

Save pages as text
  Save pages as text... saves the chosen pages' text as a text file (drag
  its icon to where it should go). Each paragraph is one line, which Edit
  and StrongED wrap to the window, with a blank line between paragraphs
  and between pages. Words split at a line's end are joined again. In an
  ebook, a sentence carried over from one page to the next is joined up. A
  scanned page has only a picture of its text: its text is saved if Folio
  has recognised it (Text recognition, with the OCR plugin), and Folio
  says so if none of the pages has any. The file is in the desktop's
  alphabet, as Copy is (Latin-1, unless the alphabet is UTF-8), so
  characters it hasn't got, such as Chinese or Japanese, become '?'.

Pictures to a PDF
  Drag a directory of pictures (JPEG, PNG, GIF, BMP, TIFF, or PBM, PGM and
  PPM, which RISC OS calls PBMPlus, &69E) to Folio's icon, or to a
  document window, and Folio makes a PDF of them, a page for each, in name
  order (Page2 comes before Page10), and opens it. Each page is the
  picture's size at its own resolution. PBM, PGM and PPM files don't
  record one, so Folio takes the usual scanning resolution (150, 200, 300,
  400 or 600 dpi) that makes the page paper-sized: a 300 dpi A4 scan comes
  out A4. A JPEG goes in as it is, not compressed again; other pictures go
  in without losing anything. The new PDF is named after the directory and
  isn't saved anywhere yet: Save... (F3) puts it where you want it, and
  Folio asks before it's closed unsaved. So: Save page pictures for a few
  pages, change the pictures in another program, drag the directory back
  to Folio, and compare.

Create PDF (joining files)
  Create PDF... on the icon bar menu opens a window to collect files in.
  Drag PDFs, pictures (JPEG, PNG, GIF, BMP, TIFF, PBM/PGM/PPM) or other
  documents Folio reads (CBZ, XPS, ebooks) into it, several at a time if
  you like; a directory dragged in adds its PDFs and pictures in name
  order. They're listed in the order they'll go. Click one to choose it;
  the buttons at the top move it Up or Down, Remove it from the list (the
  file isn't touched), Sort the list by name (Page2 before Page10) or
  Clear it. Create PDF joins them into one new PDF, which opens in a
  window called Combined, unsaved: Save... (F3) puts it where you want it.
  The Create PDF window stays open, so you can change the list and make
  another. A PDF's pages are copied just as they are (pictures, fonts,
  notes, form fields), a picture becomes a page of its own size, JPEGs
  aren't compressed again, and an ebook is laid out as Folio shows it. The
  list stays, so you can change it and make the PDF again. A PDF that
  needs a password has to be opened and saved without it first.

Scanning (network scanners)
  Scan... on the icon bar menu scans from a scanner on the network with
  eSCL ("AirScan"), which most network scanners and printer-scanners of
  the last ten years understand. No driver is needed. Type the scanner's
  IP address (such as 192.168.0.40; not a name), or click Find to look for scanners on
  your network (if it finds several, a menu of them lets you choose).
  Choose the glass (one page) or the feeder (every page in it, both sides
  if the scanner can), colour or grey, 150, 300 or 600 dpi, and A4, Letter
  or the largest area. Brightness and Contrast (-100 to 100; 0 is the
  scanner's own) are done by the scanner itself, so the pages are still its
  own JPEGs; not every scanner can, and one that can't scans as it would.
  Then click Scan. For several pages from the glass, scan the first, then
  put each next page on the glass and click Add page: it goes on the end
  of that scan (while its window is open), and Undo (Ctrl-Z) takes a page
  off again. Folio carries on while the scanner
  works, and Stop ends the scan, keeping the pages so far. The pages open
  as a new PDF called Scan, unsaved, with the scanner's JPEGs in it as they
  came; Save (F3) puts it where you want it. With the OCR plugin,
  "Recognise the text" (ticked unless you untick it) has Folio read the
  text in every page as soon as the scan opens, in the background, so it
  can be found, selected, copied and read aloud. The address is remembered.
  To check a scanner speaks eSCL, open http://<its address>/eSCL/
  ScannerCapabilities in a web browser: a page of XML means it does.
  Scanners that only accept secure (https) connections aren't supported
  yet, nor are USB scanners.

Turning, deleting, moving and inserting pages (PDFs)
  Pages on the document menu has Rotate, Delete pages..., Move pages...
  and Insert pages from file.... Rotate turns the page you pressed Menu
  over (or all pages) a quarter turn either way or a half turn: for a page
  fed in the wrong way up, or a landscape page scanned upright. Ctrl-R and
  Ctrl-L turn the page under the pointer clockwise and anticlockwise.
  Delete pages and Move pages open a small box with the page you pressed
  Menu over; type others if you like (3, or 1-3, 20, 45-50). Move puts
  them, in their order, in front of the page you type, or at the end if
  you leave that empty. Insert pages from file asks which page the new
  pages go in front of (empty: the end); then drag a PDF, a picture or
  another document Folio reads into the box, and its pages go in.
  Each change is one step: Undo (Ctrl-Z) puts it back, and Save (F3) keeps
  it. Text recognised on a page goes with the page when it moves; on a
  page that's turned it's read again. A PDF whose author doesn't allow its
  pages to be changed says so, and offers to lift that with the owner
  password.

Opening at a page
  To open a document at a given page, from an Obey file, a program or the
  command line, set Folio$OpenPage first:
      Set Folio$OpenPage 12
      Filer_Run SDFS::Disc.$.Docs.Manual/pdf
  This works whether or not Folio is running; the variable is unset once
  used. Starting Folio with Run <Folio$Dir>.!Run -page 12 <file> does the
  same. (A book's later pages are counted first, so it may take a moment.)

Links, Contents and Find
  Links have a blue line under them (untick "Underline links" in the
  Choices window to have them as the document draws them).
  Click a link to follow it: a link to another part of the document goes
  there; a web or email link opens in your browser or mail program.
  Dragging from a link selects text as usual. Navigate > Back (Ctrl-Left)
  returns to where you were, link after link.
  Navigate > Contents... lists the document's chapters and sections, with
  their page numbers; the part you're reading is highlighted. Click on one
  to go there.
  F5 (or Navigate > Go to page...) asks for a page number and goes there;
  Back returns from it as from a link.
  F4 (or Find... on the menu) opens the Find box. Type the text and press
  Return (Next) to find it; Previous goes back. Capitals don't matter.
  The search starts at the page you're reading and goes round the end of
  the document once. What it finds is selected, so you can copy it or
  look it up.

Selecting text and looking words up
  Drag with Select to select text; double-click a word to select it;
  Adjust extends the selection. A selection can run on over the next
  pages (or back over earlier ones): drag past the window's top or bottom
  and it scrolls, faster the further out you go. Ctrl-C copies it to the clipboard,
  Ctrl-A selects the whole page, Escape clears the selection.
  With one word selected, Menu shows Define "word": its meanings from
  Wiktionary (CC BY-SA) appear in a small box near the word. Copy copies
  them; clicking "Wiktionary" opens the whole entry in your browser.
  Looking words up needs the network; Folio loads the URL_Fetcher,
  AcornHTTP and AcornSSL modules from !System the first time. Words
  already looked up are kept in
  <Choices$Write>.Folio.Dict and work offline.

Folio reopens each document at the page where you left it, at the same
scale, pages across, and window size and place (kept in
<Choices$Write>.Folio.Places). Pages are drawn by a background thread, so
the desktop keeps going while a heavy page loads.

Ebooks are laid out to a 400 x 600 point page at 11 point text. Folio
counts a book's pages in the background the first time, then remembers
them in <Choices$Write>.Folio.Layouts, so it opens at once next time.

Needs a 32 thousand or more colour screen mode in this test build.

If anything goes wrong, the file <Wimp$ScrapDir>.FolioLog says what
Folio did. It's started afresh each time Folio runs, so copy it somewhere
before running Folio again. Please report problems, with the log, either
in Folio's thread on the RISC OS Open forum (forum.riscosopen.org), or as
an issue at github.com/adyoull/riscos-folio/issues (attach the log, or
paste it in).

